      "id": 1,
      "text": "Steelville was founded in 1835 when James Steel sold 40 acres to the county court, and the town was platted that same month.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21,
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steelville%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s name honors landowner James Steel, who also operated a trading post and helped lay out the city.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steelville%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1834, Levi Lane Snelson built the Snelson-Brinker House just outside Steelville, later listed on the National Register.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24,
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steelville%2C_Missouri"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The historic Dillard Mill, a preserved grist mill, offers a glimpse into Steelville’s early industrial past.",
